<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Futong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1363">1363</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Futong was a leader of the Red Turban rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shantong">Han Shantong</a> was killed in 1351, he continued the revolt, and in 1355 he enthroned Han's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> as emperor of a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> at Bozhou. He held real power in the regime until he was killed in 1363 during an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>'s forces.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Liu Futong was recruited in 1351 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shantong">Han Shantong</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> sectarian leader in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Earlier that year the Yuan official Toqto'a had mobilised 150,000 poor farmers to redirect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and Liu drew many of these disgruntled workers into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban Rebellion</a>, which grew greatly from 1351. After Han Shantong's capture and execution by Yuan forces, Liu escaped with Han's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> to Yingzhou, where he set up his base, and in 1352 he conquered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a> was proclaimed emperor of the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> in March 1355. Though Han was a figurehead, his legitimacy and Liu's own adherence to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> sect held the Northern Red Turbans together, which their southern counterparts lacked. In June 1358 Liu's army captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, the first capital of the Song dynasty, and made it the new capital. A surge of support followed: Liu raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, took parts of Anhui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and sent his general Mao Gui on an expedition against the Yuan capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Yuan commander Chaghan Temur retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in September 1359 and drove Liu first to Yingzhou, then to Bozhou and then to Anfeng, ending the expansion of the Northern Red Turbans. By 1362 they had disappeared, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, the future founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, who kept a nominal allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a>'s Song regime. In February 1363 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>'s general Lü Zhen captured Anfeng and killed Liu Futong.</p><p>In March 1363 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-da">Xu Da</a> rescued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-liner">Han Lin'er</a>. The campaign was risky, since Zhu was fighting his main rival, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-youliang">Chen Youliang</a>, at the same time, but the historian Frederick W. Mote holds that it was necessary to keep Zhu's generals united.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liu Futong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He reigned in name only, with real power held first by <b>Liu Futong</b> and later by Zhu Yuanzhang.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-16">16 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1355 his general <b>Liu Futong</b> enthroned Han Shantong's son Han Lin'er as emperor of a restored Song at Bozhou in Anhui.</blockquote><a href="/state/han-song">Han Song</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jieshou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.26,115.36&amp;year=1353" aria-label="Jieshou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Liu Futong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4669 4715h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4669 4715h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.26,115.36&amp;year=1353">What was here in 1353? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
